# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for open-DARTS CI (helper_scripts/tsan_check.py).
#
# Every rule below suppresses a *proven* ThreadSanitizer+OpenMP runtime
# limitation, not an application bug -- see each section's comment for the
# evidence before adding to or removing from this list. Format reference:
# https://github.com/google/sanitizers/wiki/ThreadSanitizerSuppressions
#
# Deliberately does NOT use a blanket "any OpenMP-outlined frame" pattern
# (e.g. race:*_omp_fn* / race:*.omp_outlined*), even though that would catch
# every barrier-crossing artifact in one rule: the confirmed, real
# interpolator-cache bug this job exists to catch a regression of has its
# flagged access inside exactly that kind of frame too (materialize_missing_
# cache's Phase 2c, [clone ._omp_fn.0]) -- a wildcard broad enough to silence
# the false positives would also silence a real regression of the bug that
# motivated this CI job. Each rule below is scoped to a function or library
# individually verified (empirically, with a standalone TSan repro, run
# outside this codebase entirely -- see the GMRES section below) not to be
# where that bug, or one like it, would show up.

# --- OpenMP runtime internals: real function names, never application logic -
# __kmp_* (libomp) / GOMP_parallel* (libgomp) are the runtime's own thread
# pool / team dispatch code. A race entirely inside these (e.g. libomp's
# lazy suspend-mutex init, __kmp_suspend_initialize_thread vs.
# __kmp_lock_suspend_mx) is the runtime racing with itself, observed even in
# a trivial "print my thread id" program with no application code at all.
race:__kmp_*
race:GOMP_parallel
race:GOMP_parallel_start

# --- GMRES vector kernels: individually proven, not pattern-matched --------
# dot/axpy/scale (linear_solvers/src/linsolv_gmres.cpp) and block_csr_spmv_add
# were the concrete case that led to a from-scratch minimal reproduction:
# a single #pragma omp parallel region, writing into a per-thread slot of a
# plain array and reading it back on the main thread immediately after the
# region closes, reproduces under both GCC's libgomp and a standard
# (non-TSan-built) LLVM libomp -- neither runtime annotates its barrier for
# TSan -- in a ~15-line standalone program with no GMRES or engine code
# involved at all. The repro compiles cleanly and prints the correct answer;
# the "race" is real to TSan, not to the program.
race:opendarts::linear_solvers::*dot*
race:opendarts::linear_solvers::*axpy*
race:opendarts::linear_solvers::*scale*
race:opendarts::linear_solvers::*block_csr_spmv*

# --- HYPRE's own internally-parallelized routines ---------------------------
# HYPRE's AMG setup/solve chains many small OpenMP-parallelized utility
# routines (coarsening, interpolation-operator construction, relaxation,
# matvec, prefix sums) across sequential calls that reuse scratch
# vectors/matrices between them -- the same barrier-crossing shape as above,
# just inside HYPRE's own code. Traced representative examples across
# several categories and found the identical shape in every one checked; a
# smaller residual fraction was not individually verified either way.
# Deliberately broad, and a real risk-acceptance (unlike the two rules
# above, this one is NOT backed by a from-scratch proof that every possible
# race here is an artifact): no confirmed genuine HYPRE thread-safety bug
# exists as of this writing, and CI signal is better spent on open-DARTS's
# own code. To get a firmer answer independent of this rule, build against
# an LLVM libomp compiled *with* TSan support enabled (a non-default CMake
# option) and see what, if anything, still reproduces.
race:hypre_*
race:HYPRE_*

# --- init_jacobian_structure: first-touch zeroing at engine init, called once
# engine_base::init_jacobian_structure's numa_set<T> calls (first-touch
# zeroing of rows_ptr/diag_ind/cols_ind for NUMA locality) get fully inlined,
# so "numa_set" never appears as its own frame in a report -- a rule matching
# that symbol name (tried first) never fires. Scoped to this function by name
# instead: it runs exactly once per engine.init(), does nothing but CSR
# structure bookkeeping (no interpolation, no well logic), and was directly
# traced (both with ThreadSanitizer and independently under Helgrind) to the
# same barrier-crossing shape as everything else in this file -- a worker's
# now-closed first-touch write vs. the main thread's next write, immediately
# after, to the same buffer.
#
# NOT scoped this way: assemble_jacobian_array's own top-of-function
# numa_set(Jac, ...) call. That function also does the actual Jacobian
# assembly this job exists to catch a regression in, so it is not
# blanket-suppressed by name -- see the "known gap" note at the bottom of
# this file, which covers it.
race:engine_base::init_jacobian_structure*

# --- Known gap, not yet covered ---------------------------------------------
# engine_super_cpu.tpp's assemble_jacobian_array has shown two distinct race
# patterns under investigation:
#   1. A self-race at its very first line (int id = omp_get_thread_num();)
#      under GCC+libgomp, from two *different* calls reusing the same stack
#      slot -- did not reproduce under Clang+libomp, consistent with the same
#      barrier-crossing artifact as everything else in this file.
#   2. Reads of op_vals_arr/op_ders_arr for a neighboring block, racing
#      against the interpolator writing those same entries -- traced several
#      examples that also looked like the barrier-crossing shape (heap reuse
#      across sequential, non-overlapping calls), but not exhaustively, so
#      this one is not yet fully resolved either way.
# Neither is suppressed here: assemble_jacobian_array is exactly where a real
# regression of either the interpolator-cache bug or a genuinely new race
# would also show up, so this function is deliberately left uncovered rather
# than guessed at. If a CI run reproduces one of these, add a rule scoped as
# narrowly as possible (e.g. by exact source line, not the whole function)
# after re-verifying it's still the same artifact.
